Auth System API

A production-style authentication REST API: sign up with email verification, login, logout with token revocation, and forgot / reset password by email. Built with Node.js, Express and MongoDB.

Live API: https://authsystem-production-7586.up.railway.app
Interactive docs (Swagger): https://authsystem-production-7586.up.railway.app/api-docs
Features
Sign up with email verification: the account is created unverified and a confirmation link is emailed. Login is blocked until the link is opened
Automatic cleanup: accounts that are never confirmed are deleted after 24 hours (MongoDB TTL index)
Login with JWT: returns a signed token; the same error message is used for unknown email and wrong password, so accounts cannot be discovered
Real logout: the token is revoked on the server (stored in Redis until it would have expired), so a stolen or old token stops working immediately
Forgot / reset password: a single-use reset link valid for 15 minutes; the response is identical whether or not the email exists
Tokens are never stored in plain text: verification and reset tokens are random, only their SHA-256 hash is saved
Passwords hashed with bcrypt
Rate limiting backed by Redis: failed logins, sign ups and reset requests are limited per IP
Request validation with Joi: unknown fields are stripped, so a client cannot set its own role or emailVerified
Centralised error handling with a consistent JSON error format
Swagger documentation at /api-docs
Tech Stack

Node.js · Express · MongoDB (Mongoose) · Redis (ioredis) · JWT · bcrypt · Joi · Nodemailer · Swagger UI

API Endpoints

Full interactive documentation is available at /api-docs.

Method	Endpoint	Auth	Description
POST	/api/v1/auth/signup	No	Create an account and send the confirmation email
GET	/api/v1/auth/verify-email/:token	No	Confirm the email (link sent by email)
POST	/api/v1/auth/login	No	Log in and receive a JWT
POST	/api/v1/auth/logout	Bearer	Revoke the current token
POST	/api/v1/auth/forgotpassword	No	Email a password reset link
PATCH	/api/v1/auth/reset-password/:token	No	Set a new password
How It Works

Sign up and verification

POST /signup validates the body, creates the user with emailVerified: false, and emails a link containing a random token
Only the token's SHA-256 hash is stored, together with a 24-hour expiry
Opening the link (GET /verify-email/:token) sets emailVerified: true and deletes the token, so the link works once
POST /login returns 403 until the email is verified

Login and logout

POST /login checks the password with bcrypt and returns a JWT (sub = user id)
The auth middleware verifies the signature, checks that the token was not revoked, and loads the user
POST /logout stores a hash of the token in Redis with a TTL equal to its remaining lifetime. Any later request with that token gets 401

Password reset

POST /forgotpassword emails a reset link (valid 15 minutes) if the account exists, and always answers the same way
PATCH /reset-password/:token checks the hashed token and its expiry, saves the new password, and invalidates the token

Rate limits

Route	Limit	Counts
login	5 per 15 minutes	failed attempts only
signup	5 per hour	every request
forgot password	5 per hour	every request
Getting Started
Prerequisites
Node.js 18+
A MongoDB database (e.g. MongoDB Atlas free tier)
A Redis instance (e.g. Redis Cloud free tier)
A Gmail account with an App Password (requires 2-Step Verification) for sending emails
Installation
bash
git clone https://github.com/ashrafelnajjar/authSystem.git
cd authSystem
npm install

Create your environment file and fill in the values (see the table below):

bash
cp .env.example .env        # Windows PowerShell: copy .env.example .env
Run
bash
npm start

The API runs on http://localhost:5000 and the docs on http://localhost:5000/api-docs.

Try it
POST /api/v1/auth/signup with a real email address you own
Open the link in the confirmation email
POST /api/v1/auth/login, then copy the token
POST /api/v1/auth/logout with Authorization: Bearer <token>, then repeat any protected request with the same token and you get 401
Environment Variables
Variable	Description
PORT	Port to listen on (set automatically on most hosts)
APP_URL	Public URL of the API, used to build the links in emails
DB_URL	MongoDB connection string
REDIS_URL	Redis connection URL
JWT_SECRET	Secret used to sign tokens
JWT_EXPIRES_IN	Token lifetime, e.g. 7d
EMAIL_USER	Gmail address used to send emails
EMAIL_PASSWORD	Gmail App Password (16 characters)
Error Format

Every error has the same shape:

json
{ "status": "fail", "message": "email or password is incorrect!" }
Status	Meaning
400	Validation error, or invalid / expired token
401	Missing, invalid, expired or revoked token
403	Email not verified
409	Email already registered
429	Too many requests
Deployment

The API is deployed on Railway. Set the variables above in the service settings and set APP_URL to the generated domain, because it is used in the links sent by email.

